  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an integer NNN.\n\nConstruct an N×NN \\times NN×N grid (i.e. NNN rows and NNN columns) filled with N2N^2N2 integers. Let Ai,jA_{i, j}Ai,j​ represent the element in iii-th row and jjj-th column.\n\nDefine Vi,jV_{i, j}Vi,j​ be the set of all elements Ax,yA_{x, y}Ax,y​ such that **exactly one** of x=ix = ix=i and y=jy = jy=j holds. In other words, Vi,jV_{i, j}Vi,j​ is the set of all elements in the same row or column, excluding (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) itself.\n\nThe grid must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n\n0≤Ai,j≤N20 \\le A_{i, j} \\le N^20≤Ai,j​≤N2\nAi,j=MEX(Vi,j)†A_{i, j} = MEX(V_{i, j}) ^{\\dagger}Ai,j​=MEX(Vi,j​)†\nIt can be proven that a valid grid always exists. Find any valid grid.\n\n†^{\\dagger}† MEX(S)MEX(S)MEX(S) is defined as the smallest non-negative integer not present in SSS. The mex of an empty set is defined to be 000.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of input contains a single integer NNN.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output NNN lines each with NNN integers. The jjj-th integer on the iii-th line should be Ai,jA_{i, j}Ai,j​. You are allowed to print any valid grid.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤301 \\le T \\le 301≤T≤30",
      "1≤N≤301 \\le N \\le 301≤N≤30"
    ],
